#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25975c9f-0706-5931-944b-cbf55f182498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371320} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d60ae4-1619-577d-b07c-10673b057727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371321} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5f6465-4768-5a68-bff5-a66b32ecef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371322} {:answer/id #uuid "41e2d886-d9dd-5f3c-9d82-437bf3ca24fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371323} {:answer/id #uuid "d08a0f98-9272-54d2-8beb-7fd66302a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d60ae4-1619-577d-b07c-10673b057727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371321} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0a09dd-e11e-5e1b-aeb9-0975801242da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3302b4c-3bdd-5b04-9e3e-aac900425c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96120" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Σ$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix\n- $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $5\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $Σ$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be diagonal with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal. The main diagonal may contain zeroes.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08090adf-a2d1-5e61-9ed0-7da111463256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371702} {:answer/id #uuid "86c76c48-0c28-52d9-baaa-98980d025994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371703} {:answer/id #uuid "adfbd491-4e02-5b18-b24a-e12752dbf336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371704} {:answer/id #uuid "4f51a20a-b3e8-5276-ba7f-c9e4df9a6459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371705} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0d4d46-a9c9-526f-aafc-8ff655040c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08090adf-a2d1-5e61-9ed0-7da111463256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b211404b-8e46-5fe3-a810-eb0ddf38f3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2b565dd-1238-5428-a4d5-150ac346f6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124332" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=1-v,\\,y=u+2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-v, \\\\ u+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-v \\\\ u\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8f2eb75-5fbe-52ed-b356-8045b9540828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372106} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd94c33-aeb0-5563-aff6-e9e2537403da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372107} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5a2b86-ea45-589a-a53f-3686a423ab4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372108} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a985c3-95a7-5912-a02d-f83ee9350a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372109} {:answer/id #uuid "f7533886-ffcd-5976-9a10-4f324458c1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8a985c3-95a7-5912-a02d-f83ee9350a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372109} :answer-field/id #uuid "a90d9bb7-a4cf-512e-8524-d22df247be4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71772efd-ca65-51b0-8691-66ebdb0248c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162556" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{-y}\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{-y}\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2x{e}^{-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-2x{e}^{-y}+2x{e}^{-y}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005a38b0-34a2-525b-a035-6cbbce837358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372113} {:answer/id #uuid "5abbdaed-9779-50b2-8c8d-5084b5a9bf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372114} {:answer/id #uuid "97dfa2ab-7a34-5e63-9a7f-79408eaaac00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372115} {:answer/id #uuid "1f557107-5d5e-5c5f-a28f-09a65e3b6d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372116} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f97bba-4166-543b-a293-147e921854b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "005a38b0-34a2-525b-a035-6cbbce837358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372113} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08aece8-45d3-544a-bbe8-949ea71a0aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6ad078-18b9-512b-9d01-0a1f7199a6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162557" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i-2{y}^{2}\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true? \n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any closed curve $C.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i-2{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=-2{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4y-4y=0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are also true. Indeed, they are equivalent to statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0134bca0-d48c-5ff1-b3ed-1454f1a1447d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372120} {:answer/id #uuid "4822ea8b-0498-5a3c-b988-4ca5a02f3bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372121} {:answer/id #uuid "176a8b6c-8fe7-57ad-a39c-c4e999bee891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372122} {:answer/id #uuid "592a13bc-c7e7-5990-940c-24644902d3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372123} {:answer/id #uuid "9819cdca-4778-5504-ad6e-72986ac59010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9819cdca-4778-5504-ad6e-72986ac59010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372124} :answer-field/id #uuid "d048bd7c-33a0-5c32-b5df-0c28051e7e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acd7f081-2dbf-5ec3-95f4-c384566f110c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162565" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field\n- Given two arbitrary curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ we have ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ is guaranteed only for curves with the same start and endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29fb2cb-2dbb-533b-bfbc-35dedefe64c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372643} {:answer/id #uuid "7798118d-4162-57b1-9d43-e0019f004b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372644} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0525ce-e645-59d8-bdcf-5f94890d65c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372645} {:answer/id #uuid "9b96fe1a-4dac-57c6-830f-9259e813041d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372646} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee4f464-7cf3-5141-b917-db76f5a484cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ee4f464-7cf3-5141-b917-db76f5a484cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372647} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5c4cbe-6fc4-557c-90ae-7db2c270cd63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6ea5569-1b38-5840-af43-7864b3cc3a00" :question/math-academy-id "q-333551" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.50.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6cd6819-3d4f-5d36-b01a-4a8f9b118206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373542} {:answer/id #uuid "46169318-8608-5ab9-93d4-86318e8fbf87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373543} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3f3711-3ac9-50b4-a9b8-6b639948d5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373544} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1ab5bd-179e-5f8a-8a5d-aae8ab35ffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373545} {:answer/id #uuid "86aa3e2a-3380-5ceb-893a-188da5e730ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86aa3e2a-3380-5ceb-893a-188da5e730ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373546} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed24b82-e248-5d47-8d98-8441865e359f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e3aa161-9904-5811-8bd5-9983d559a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107047" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28fb9097-326f-5f92-9a3a-22b2117f1e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374277} {:answer/id #uuid "c588b793-4074-5338-897b-061af8a18274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374278} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7a241e-e305-5c45-aa3b-334d577afee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374279} {:answer/id #uuid "58588e80-bf67-5326-b31f-52ad6b3d26c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374280} {:answer/id #uuid "06ad02c7-a4e1-5b1c-a14f-2951a7f47b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fb9097-326f-5f92-9a3a-22b2117f1e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374277} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3a2997-a72b-576c-ab37-310ab6d75c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fbffaaa-23d8-507a-8897-e781a41c0406" :question/math-academy-id "q-174356" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={n\\in N∣2n+1<9},\\,B={n\\in N∣n\\ge 4}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A={1,2,3}$\n- $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the elements of $A$ are positive integers that satisfy the inequality \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+1 & <9 \\\\ 2n & <8 \\\\ n & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={n\\in N∣2n+1<9} \\\\  & ={n\\in N∣n<4} \\\\  & ={1,2,3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={n\\in N∣n\\ge 4} \\\\  & ={4,5,6,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B={1,2,3}\\cap {4,5,6,…}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bbbc0b-ad0d-5d95-8b72-4d1c8da41f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374284} {:answer/id #uuid "0f182aa7-cd44-5fe4-9113-fdb53219b246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374285} {:answer/id #uuid "b81fcb99-ba33-5fc0-9ba0-5fe4b9feac3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374286} {:answer/id #uuid "4c01e557-bb9a-50a3-966b-68f0667388c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374287} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcec960-3d44-57b5-886c-6e2c2c11e9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c01e557-bb9a-50a3-966b-68f0667388c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374287} :answer-field/id #uuid "6edf5688-4bce-54b5-a9d2-aa20e178234f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e6d5c0d-f5b8-5d41-acfe-dd83480488ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-174369" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={2n∣n\\in N},\\,B={{2}^{n}∣n\\in N}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B={2,4,8,16,…}$\n- $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,16,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,8,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & ={2,4,6,8,…}\\text{ }\\cap {2,4,8,16,…} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,…} \\\\  & \\ne ∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the intersection is not empty, the two sets are not disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a75bed52-3210-5186-b0a2-6327a4e867bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374291} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7624ee-b577-5c08-9483-211bef34566c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374292} {:answer/id #uuid "8288ced1-b65e-589b-ba15-21f457e8303f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374293} {:answer/id #uuid "c528435e-d916-5f10-9a2d-3a71249cff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374294} {:answer/id #uuid "954659ac-50d9-5b84-8951-cbd90c01f9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7624ee-b577-5c08-9483-211bef34566c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c713fc4-0c22-59b0-977e-74b521261f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f863cc29-74d9-5551-a15b-2146bb98a0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-174372" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\},\\,B=\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B=\\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\} \\\\  & \\ne \\{-1,-2,-3,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{5n∣n\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…\\}\\cap \\{-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6efdda93-e488-52a9-b295-ea564a97fa74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375633} {:answer/id #uuid "91700a18-c990-5969-9161-60b52a4708a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375634} {:answer/id #uuid "45bc3fe5-3764-5087-a16f-9bae2c1e651d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375635} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fb98c7-efc1-544d-83fe-b3a5be20f7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375636} {:answer/id #uuid "36d6a570-45e1-5e04-b7d8-6e8debd788f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6efdda93-e488-52a9-b295-ea564a97fa74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375633} :answer-field/id #uuid "377f97f1-5b53-5212-9e2e-8673b4f6d3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0b7a4cb-746e-55f4-a074-7c8a26769a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-103482" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- **If** $-5>-2,$ **then** $-2<-1.$\n- **If** $-5<-2,$ **then** $-2<-1.$\n- **If** $-5>-2,$ **then** $-2>-1.$" :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent $-5>-2$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. The antecedent $-5<-2$ is true, and the consequent $-2<-1$ is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent $-5>-2$ is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06271d57-0934-56eb-96a3-ed1870802e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375640} {:answer/id #uuid "1d04450e-1ff2-5843-b13c-4c5390fa92e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375641} {:answer/id #uuid "878c1638-baba-553a-a018-9f2643bd35af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375642} {:answer/id #uuid "cfae8cea-0e54-5226-8425-c7d769e17697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375643} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfdd82f-d34b-595b-ba15-afb3566e61f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06271d57-0934-56eb-96a3-ed1870802e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375640} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c13b258-02bc-571c-be22-4efddeb6b233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5661c06a-c66c-5923-befb-278bbbf8f25e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105331" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/f3/f3b1df5f948603edcc1095da9215145cba21c7aebbdfdd6ab5cacecb5810efc7.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7bc1b41e3ca8aba5c1a5658ec421f7358041c57dea7c1ef45fe265c62664f097.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ satisfy the equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba4ba05-5975-5600-91df-d4ebfc647f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375647} {:answer/id #uuid "e445dc1c-7bdc-584c-8de4-0830a84c0097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375648} {:answer/id #uuid "95f17243-2279-538f-881c-66982c2023e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375649} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb08f16-51f2-54c1-8fa9-6bd2c24d500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375650} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f87a35-83a7-5600-83d3-0210752e8c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f17243-2279-538f-881c-66982c2023e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e607c8-1e9d-51ff-8b16-34e90c6a063d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab42b0ba-ecca-5e90-984d-305864760335" :question/math-academy-id "q-105336" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(3,2)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(3,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/67/67d1f23f424561c69973fe5f08ad3ee3a03346f555db8f00fb71406c02a9cdf9.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,2)$ and $Q(3,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/0b/0b3e72b3a65241c4e03716a9c0b534d687c305a92ceef0b92d5c6df8c1b82953.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ satisfy the inequality $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ do not satisfy the equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382969c5-f931-58f2-b34f-f69c2b1c521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375946} {:answer/id #uuid "5db024b5-a6f3-567f-9c92-7456e6fb5fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375947} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d0328-a468-5a15-9678-b92406cd3050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375948} {:answer/id #uuid "fd21305c-6989-5793-9c69-05cfeb8426d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375949} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8dc819-2840-5b8d-903c-81838d8dc963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d0328-a468-5a15-9678-b92406cd3050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375948} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcf0b75-42a2-57a9-b53b-de57c7f819c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8048be79-9af8-51c0-a0c1-5b68f549139c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139986" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}?\n$$\n\n- The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcfdfa9a-c21a-507e-b09f-a1d4b65712f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375974} {:answer/id #uuid "e49de3d3-def2-5578-874d-f535e8cc3039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375975} {:answer/id #uuid "4069c094-9b83-5c5b-8d44-9be651df575f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375976} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1c2fe9-7057-5bc1-be1d-6fb21b759755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375977} {:answer/id #uuid "e595bf2c-f053-5da2-876a-64f8e451b3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcfdfa9a-c21a-507e-b09f-a1d4b65712f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375974} :answer-field/id #uuid "b31d7e94-510f-5d62-993c-a336f4806810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "017db163-2592-561e-a975-d762dc265a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-142542" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}$\n- $A$ is  invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=5,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 5$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94a365a5-fad1-58b8-844f-2311d35743ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376084} {:answer/id #uuid "dd753bd6-2a23-525f-af72-81d4a9366e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376085} {:answer/id #uuid "79a6fe55-5e87-5f11-9942-9e6a4f2ac075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376086} {:answer/id #uuid "67fa9fd5-d726-5947-a5ac-c1a483f1f2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376087} {:answer/id #uuid "3f19879b-57b2-50ee-8248-c2b6ba94ff3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fa9fd5-d726-5947-a5ac-c1a483f1f2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376087} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a40b241-bae3-5aa3-8731-9d1da68b6fd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80400990-b978-5e6c-95c6-54cde07862e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158534" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le x+2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=x+2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/3b/3b0505f0c1fb295665dd64c9d0f26f23960b638ef0e1d414c7ad306a89154dfb.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,4)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/62/623f00c423c7a846c52537d58c071c730083ba963fb488dbedbb76637ea9cc8c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the equation $y=x+2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le x+4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}]}
